<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam Ferguson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1723">1723</a>–<a href="/years/1816">1816</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam Ferguson was a Scottish philosopher and historian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>, professor at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>. His Essay on the History of Civil Society of 1767 traced how societies develop from rude to commercial stages and warned that commerce and the division of labour could weaken civic virtue. He is often counted among the founders of modern sociology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-ferguson.jpg" alt="Adam Ferguson" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joshua Reynolds · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ProfAdamFerguson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Scottish Enlightenment flourished after the union: David Hume, Adam Smith, whose Wealth of Nations appeared in 1776, and <b>Adam Ferguson</b> shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while Samuel Johnson’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, Samuel Richardson and Henry Fielding helped establish modern English prose…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Logierait, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.65,-3.68&amp;year=1783" aria-label="Logierait on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4470 239 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adam Ferguson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4920 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4920 509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.65,-3.68&amp;year=1783">What was here in 1783? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every step and every movement of the multitude, even in what are termed enlightened ages, are made with equal blindness to the future; and nations stumble upon establishments, which are indeed the result of human action, but not the execution of any human design.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on the History of Civil Society (1767), part 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8646/pg8646-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Manufactures, accordingly, prosper most where the mind is least consulted, and where the workshop may, without any great effort of imagination, be considered as an engine, the parts of which are men.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on the History of Civil Society (1767), part 4 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8646/pg8646-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Mankind are to be taken in groupes, as they have always subsisted. The history of the individual is but a detail of the sentiments and the thoughts he has entertained in the view of his species…&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on the History of Civil Society (1767), part 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8646/pg8646-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If the pretensions to equal justice and freedom should terminate in rendering every class equally servile and mercenary, we make a nation of helots, and have no free citizens.&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay on the History of Civil Society (1767), part 5 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8646/pg8646-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
